          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The term
            %em double field theory
            has come to be used for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"}> prequantum field theory
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"}> quantum field theory
            ) on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-folds"} T-folds
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doubled+geometries"}> doubled geometries
            ) hence for “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"}> T-duality
            \-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance"} equivariant
            field theory”.
          %h2#proposal_of_formalization_in_parahermitian_geometry Proposal of formalization in para-Hermitian geometry
          %p The most popular proposal of formalization of double field theory is in the context of para-Hermitian geometry.
          %h3#doubled_space_as_parahermitian_manifold Doubled space as para-Hermitian manifold
          %p
            The use of para-hermitian geometry in Double Field Theory was introduced by Izu Vaisman in (
            %a{:href => "#Vaisman"}> Vai 12
            ), then by David Svoboda (
            %a{:href => "#Svoboda"}> Svo 18
            ).
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An
              %em almost para-complex manifold
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              equipped with a vector bundle endomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\in\mathrm{End}(T M)
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^2=1
              and its
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ±
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm 1
              eigenbundles
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^\pm M
              have same rank.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em para-complex projectors
              are the canonical projectors onto
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^\pm M
              defined by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mo ±
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ±
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_\pm = \frac{1}{2}(1\pm F)
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ±
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm
              %em -para-complex manifold
              is an almost para-complex manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,F)
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^\pm M
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is Frobenius integrable as a distribution. A
              %em para-complex
              manifold is a manifold that is both
              = succeed "-para-complex" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
              = succeed "-para-complex." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              A doubled manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              equipped with the
              = succeed "-structure" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi O
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo ,
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(d,d)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              carries a natural almost para-hermitian structure. On patches
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              with coordinates
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi μ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x^\mu,\tilde{x}_\mu)
              we have the canonical para-complex structure
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi μ
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi μ
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi F
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mi μ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\frac{\partial}{\partial x^\mu} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x^\mu},\,\, F\frac{\partial}{\partial \tilde{x}_\mu} = - \frac{\partial}{\partial \tilde{x}_\mu}
            %p with eigenbundles
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi T
                          %mo +
                        %mi U
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ⟨
                        %mfrac
                          %mo ∂
                          %mrow
                            %mo ∂
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mi μ
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^+U=\big\langle\frac{\partial}{\partial x^\mu}\big\rangle
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi T
                          %mo −
                        %mi U
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ⟨
                        %mfrac
                          %mo ∂
                          %mrow
                            %mo ∂
                            %msub
                              %mover
                                %mi x
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                              %mi μ
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^-U=\big\langle\frac{\partial}{\partial \tilde{x}_\mu}\big\rangle
                  ,
            %p with associated foliations
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℱ
                          %mo +
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi μ
                        %mo =
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} const
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_+=\{x^\mu = \mathrm{const}\}
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℱ
                          %mo −
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mi μ
                        %mo =
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} const
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_-=\{\tilde{x}_\mu = \mathrm{const}\}
                  \.
            %p
              In analogy with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+geometry"} complex geometry
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo ,
                        %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{r,s}(M)
              as the space of sections of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo +
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %msup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo −
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^r(T^+M)\wedge\Lambda^s(T^-M)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo =
                        %mi r
                        %mo +
                        %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo ,
                        %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Omega^k(M)=\bigoplus_{k=r+s}\Omega^{r,s}(M)
            %p
              Let us call
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo ,
                        %mi s
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo +
                        %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo ,
                        %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^{r,s}:\Omega^{r+s}(M)\rightarrow\Omega^{r,s}(M)
              the canonical projector induced by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mo ±
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_\pm
            %p
              In analogy with complex geometry, there are
              = succeed ":" do
                %em para-Dolbeault operators
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mo +
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mrow
                            %mi r
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mi s
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi r
                            %mo ,
                            %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⟶
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi r
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}^+=\pi^{r+1,s}\circ\mathrm{d} : \Omega^{r,s}(M)\longrightarrow \Omega^{r+1,s}(M)
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mo −
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mrow
                            %mi r
                            %mo ,
                            %mi s
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi r
                            %mo ,
                            %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⟶
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi r
                            %mo ,
                            %mi s
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}^-=\pi^{r,s+1}\circ\mathrm{d} : \Omega^{r,s}(M)\longrightarrow \Omega^{r,s+1}(M)
                  \.
            %p with properties:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mo +
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %msup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mo −
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d} = \mathrm{d}^+ + \mathrm{d}^-
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mo ±
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathrm{d}^\pm)^2= 0
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mo +
                        %msup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mo −
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %msup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mo −
                        %msup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mo +
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}^+\mathrm{d}^-+\mathrm{d}^-\mathrm{d}^+ =0
                  \.
            %p
              We can also define
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+derivatives"} Lie derivatives
              on the eigenbundles
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^\pm M
              by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo ±
                      %mo ±
                    %mi ξ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mo ±
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo ±
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo ±
                    %msup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mo ±
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ξ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{L}^\pm_{X_\pm}\xi = (\mathrm{d}^\pm\iota_{X_\pm} + \iota_{X_\pm}\mathrm{d}^\pm)\xi
            %p
              for any vector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mo ±
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\pm\in\Gamma(T^\pm M)
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ξ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                          %mo ,
                          %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi\in\Omega^{r,s}(M)
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An
              %em almost para-hermitian manifold
              is an almost para-complex manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              equipped with a compatible metric, i.e a symmetric tensor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta\in\mathrm{Sym}^2(T M)
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta(F\cdot\,,F\cdot\,)=-\eta(\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,)
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The contraction with the metric
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              defines two isomorphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ±
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ∓
                    %mi M
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi^\pm : T^\pm M \rightarrow (T^\mp M)^\ast
            %p by
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo ±
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo ±
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msubsup
                          %mi X
                          %mo ±
                          %mo ♭
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^\pm(X_\pm) =  X_\pm^\flat
                  and
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo ±
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi ξ
                          %mo ±
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi ξ
                          %mo ♯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi^\pm)^{-1}(\xi_\pm)=\xi^\sharp
            %p
              that map a vector in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^\pm M
              to a
              = succeed "-form" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ∓
                    %mi M
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T^\mp M)^\ast
              and vice-versa. We used the notation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♭
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ♯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat,\sharp
              for the musical isomorphisms induced by the metric
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T M
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo *
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^\ast M
            %p This can be used to define a new couple of isomorphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Φ
                      %mo ±
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Phi^\pm : T M \longrightarrow T^\pm M\oplus(T^\pm M)^\ast
            %p
              that maps a vector a vector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=X_++X_-
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mo ±
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo ±
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\pm\in T^\pm M
              into
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mo +
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %msubsup
                        %mi X
                        %mo −
                        %mo ♭
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_+ + X_-^\flat
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em para-hermitian manifold
              is an almost para-hermitian manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi η
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,\eta,F)
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,F)
              is a para-complex manifold.
          %h3#foliated_courant_algebroid_and_spacetime Foliated Courant algebroid and spacetime
          %p
            Given a
            = succeed "-para-hermitian" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi η
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,\eta,F)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo +
                      %mi M
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big(T^+ M,[\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,]_+,1_{T^+M}\big)
            where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the skew-symmetric bracket
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo +
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi T
                            %mo +
                          %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,]_+:=[\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,]|_{T^+ M}
            %li
              %p
                the anchor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi T
                            %mo ±
                          %mi M
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi T
                          %mi M
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi T
                            %mo ±
                          %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{T^\pm M}:=1_{T M}|_{T^\pm M}
                are the restrictions of Lie bracket and identity of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T M
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is
            = succeed "-para-hermitian" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^+M
            is integrable and therefore it is the tangent bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                  %mo =
                  %mi T
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^+M = T\mathcal{F}_\pm
            of a foliation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_+
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_+
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo +
                      %mi M
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi T
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %msub
                        %mi ℱ
                        %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %msub
                        %mi ℱ
                        %mo +
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\big(T^+ M,[\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,]_+,1_{T^+ M}\big) = \big(T\mathcal{F}_+,[\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,]_{T\mathcal{F}_+},1_{T\mathcal{F}_+}\big).
          %p
            There is a natural Courant algebroid structure on the bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^+ M\oplus(T^+ M)^\ast
            with skew-symmetric pairing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi β
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                  %mi β
                  %mo −
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi Y
                    %mo +
                  %mi α
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mi Y
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [X+\alpha,Y+\beta]_{+} = [X,Y]_+ + \mathcal{L}_X^+\beta -\mathcal{L}_Y^+\alpha +\mathrm{d}_+(\iota_Y\alpha)
          %p and symmetric pairing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi β
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo +
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mi X
                  %mi β
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mi Y
                  %mi α
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle X+\alpha,Y+\beta\rangle_+ = \iota_X\beta + \iota_Y\alpha.
          %p
            The isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo +
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo −
                  %mi M
                  %mo =
                  %mi T
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi^+:T^+ M\oplus (T^+ M)^\ast\rightarrow T^+ M\oplus T^- M = T M
            previously defined induces a Courant algebroid isomorphism and hence a Courant algebroid structure on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and a skew-symmetric pairing on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T M
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} [
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mo +
                    %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Y
                    %mo −
                    %mo ♭
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mo +
                    %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi X
                    %mo −
                    %mo ♭
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} ]
                    %mo ♯
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [[X_++X_-,Y_++Y_-]]_+ := [X_+,Y_+] + \Big[\mathcal{L}_{X_+}^+ Y_-^\flat -\mathcal{L}_{Y_+}^+ X_-^\flat +\mathrm{d}_+\big(\eta(X_-,Y_+)\big)\Big]^\sharp,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi T
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in T M
            is split in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mo ±
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mo ±
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\pm=P_\pm X
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is assumed
              = succeed "-para-hermitian," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo +
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo +
                    %mi M
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^+ M\oplus (T^+ M)^\ast
              can be written as
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %msub
                        %mi ℱ
                        %mo +
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo *
                      %msub
                        %mi ℱ
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\mathcal{F}_+\oplus T^\ast\mathcal{F}_+
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T M
              and the generalized tangent bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %msub
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo +
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\mathcal{F}_+\oplus T^\ast\mathcal{F}_+
              of the foliation. In other terms para-hermitian geometry of the doubled manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              reduces to Generalized Geometry of physical spacetime.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The same argument can be clearly applied to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo −
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^-M
              too.
          %h3#cbracket C-bracket
          %p
            In previous section we assumed that the
            = succeed "-eigenbundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo +
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^+M
            is integrable. This is equivalent to assuming that there exists a well defined foliation that can be interpreted as the physical spacetime. However it is possible to construct a more general bracket that does not require such an assumption, but only an almost para-complex structure. Therefore it works even when a global physical spacetime foliation is not defined. This is achieved by Vaisman with the definition of
            %em C-bracket
            by using a generalization of the notion of Levi-Civita connection (look (
            %a{:href => "#Vaisman"}> Vai 2012
            )).
          %p In the special case of an (integrable) para-hermitian manifold C-bracket is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} C
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi X
                  %mi β
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi Y
                  %mi α
                  %mo +
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi α
                    %mo *
                  %mi Y
                  %mo −
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi β
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [[ X+\alpha,Y+\beta ]]_{\mathrm{C}} = [X,Y] + \mathcal{L}_X\beta -\mathcal{L}_Y\alpha + \mathrm{d}\big(\eta(X+\alpha,Y+\beta)\big) +
                  [\alpha,\beta]^\ast + \mathcal{L}^\ast_\alpha Y -\mathcal{L}^\ast_\beta X - \mathrm{d}^\ast\big(\eta(X+\alpha,Y+\beta)\big)
          %p
            where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}_X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}
            are usual Lie bracket, Lie derivative and differential on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %msub
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\mathcal{F}_+
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,]^\ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi α
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}^\ast_\alpha
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}^\ast
            are Lie bracket, Lie derivative and differential on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo +
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\mathcal{F}_+^\ast
            induced by the former ones.
          %h3#analogy_with_geometric_quantization Analogy with geometric quantization
          %p
            The analogy between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            and DFT was firstly noticed by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Berman"} David Berman
          %p
            Given a symplectic manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,\omega)
            there exist couples of lagrangian foliations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_+,\mathcal{F}_-
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi T
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\forall X,Y\in T\mathcal{F}_\pm, \: \omega(X,Y)=0.
          %p
            For example for a symplectic space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi μ
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R}^{2d},\omega=\mathrm{d}x^\mu\wedge\mathrm{d}p_\mu)
            we can have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo +
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} const
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_+ = \{x^\mu = \mathrm{const} \}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo −
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi μ
                    %mo =
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} const
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_-= \{p_\mu = \mathrm{const} \}
          %p
            Heuristically, in geometrical quantization we make a choice of a couple of lagrangian foliations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_\pm
            to “select” a physical spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_+
            from the whole symplectic-covariant theory on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %p
            Similarly in DFT, when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is an (integrable) para-hermitian manifold we make a choice of a couple of lagrangian foliations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_\pm
            to “select” a physical spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_+
            from the whole T-duality-covariant theory on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %h2#relation_between_parahermitian_geometry_and_gerbes Relation between para-Hermitian geometry and gerbes
          %p
            Let us start from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi:P\rightarrow M
            on a
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi U
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi U
                    %mo ×
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P|_U \cong U\times \mathbf{B}U(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}U(1)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+2-group"} circle 2-group
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is an open set. If we consider an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d\rightarrow U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %mo ×
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d\times \mathbf{B}U(1)\rightarrow U\times \mathbf{B}U(1)
            which extends the former. In other words the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            is locally modelled on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-group"} Lie 2-group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi d
                    %mo ×
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d\times \mathbf{B}U(1)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %mo ×
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d\times \mathbf{B}U(1)
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi d
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi b
                    %mi 𝔲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d \oplus \mathbf{b}\mathfrak{u}(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            for this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
            will be just an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+n-algebras"} Lie n-algebras
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝔞
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathfrak{a} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^d \oplus \mathbf{b}\mathfrak{u}(1)
          %p
            which is surjective in the lowest degree. What choice of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            can we make?
          %p
            To find a working atlas we can consider the dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebras"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast: \mathrm{CE}(\mathbb{R}^d \oplus \mathbf{b}\mathfrak{u}(1)) \longrightarrow \mathrm{CE}(\mathfrak{a}).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{CE}(\mathbb{R}^d \oplus \mathbf{b}\mathfrak{u}(1))
            has a degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            generator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            which is annihilated by the differential, i.e.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d} b=0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega := f^\ast b
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            is a of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            element which satisfies the equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d} \omega = 0 .
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d
            must be a subalgebra of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            must be rotation-invariant, we have no other choice than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}=\mathbb{R}^d \oplus (\mathbb{R}^d)^\ast
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi μ
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi μ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega = \mathrm{d}\tilde{x}_\mu \wedge \mathrm{d}x^\mu.
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}\tilde{x}_\mu
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}x^\mu
            are respectively generators of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{CE}((\mathbb{R}^d)^\ast)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} CE
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{CE}(\mathbb{R}^d)
          %p
            The vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d \oplus (\mathbb{R}^d)^\ast
            equipped with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi μ
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi μ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} LI
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega = \mathrm{d}\tilde{x}_\mu \wedge \mathrm{d}x^\mu \in \Omega_{\mathrm{LI}}^1(\mathbb{R}^d \oplus (\mathbb{R}^d)^\ast)
            is nothing but the local space on which para-Hermitian geometry is modelled. On the other side of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝔞
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathfrak{a} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^d \oplus \mathbf{b}\mathfrak{u}(1)
            we have an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+Minkowski+spacetime"} extended Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbes"} bundle gerbes
            are modelled.
          %p
            This relation is introduced in (
            %a{:href => "#Alfonsi20"}> Alf20
            ).
          %h2#relation_with_nongeometry Relation with non-geometry
          %p
            Double field theory is supposed to formalize the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-geometric+backgrounds"} non-geometric backgrounds
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
          %h3#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-fold"}> T-fold
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Exotic+branes"} Exotic branes
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doubled+geometry"}> doubled geometry
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+geometry"} generalized geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-geometry"} non-geometry
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-fold"} T-fold
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exotic+brane"} exotic brane
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+field+theory"}> exceptional field theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+generalized+geometry"} exceptional generalized geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Scherk-Schwarz+reduction"}> generalized Scherk-Schwarz reduction
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauged+supergravity"} gauged supergravity
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+hierarchy"} tensor hierarchy
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Original articles:
          %p
            The idea of “doubled spacetime geometry” is a variant of the idea of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-folds"} T-folds
          %ul
            %li#Hull06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Hull"}> Chris Hull
              ,
              %em Doubled geometry and T-folds
              JHEP0707:080,2007 (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0605149"}> arXiv:hep-th/0605149
              )
          %p
            The terminology “section condition” in double field hteory originates with the following article (which is more generally concerned with a kind of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+field+theory"} exceptional field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+S.+Berman"}> David S. Berman
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Malcolm+J.+Perry"} Malcolm J. Perry
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Generalized Geometry and M theory
                %strong 2011
                74 (2011) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.1763v4"}> arXiv:1008.1763v4
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/JHEP06(2011)074"} doi:10.1007/JHEP06(2011)074
              %blockquote
                %p
                  \&amp;lbrack;p 3:&amp;rbrack; “One half of the [doubled] space is the usual geometry and the other is its T-dual. Of course one also must define a
                  %em section
                  on the doubled space to relate it to a particular duality frame in order to retrieve some kind of conventional spacetime picture.”
                %p &amp;lbrack;p. 4:&amp;rbrack; “a kind of section condition describing how one takes a section through the extended space that results in our usual spacetime view. […] there are many possible ways of taking a section through the doubled space and these are of course related by T-duality transformations. The absence of a globally defined section is also possible and it is this possibility that gives rise to so called nongeometric backgrounds such as T-folds where local geometric patches are related by transition function given by T-duality transformations. ”
          %p The coinage of the term “double field theory” for field theory on such doubled geometry goes back to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Hull"}> Chris Hull
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barton+Zwiebach"} Barton Zwiebach
              = succeed "," do
                %em Double Field Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0904.4664"} arXiv:0904.4664
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Gerardo Aldazabal, Diego Marques, Carmen Nunez:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Double Field Theory: A Pedagogical Review
                %strong 30
                (2013) 163001 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.1907"}> arXiv:1305.1907
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1088/0264-9381/30/16/163001"} doi:10.1088/0264-9381/30/16/163001
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Olaf+Hohm"}> Olaf Hohm
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dieter+L%C3%BCst"} Dieter Lüst
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barton+Zwiebach"} Barton Zwiebach
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Spacetime of Double Field Theory: Review, Remarks, and Outlook
                %strong 61
                10 (2013) 926-966 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.2977"}> arXiv:1309.2977
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/prop.201300024"} doi:10.1002/prop.201300024
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Falk+Hassler"}> Falk Hassler
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Osten"} David Osten
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuho+Sakatani"} Yuho Sakatani
              %em Duality covariant curvatures for the heterotic string
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.17893"}> arXiv:2412.17893
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#formalization_in_parahermitian_geometry Formalization in para-Hermitian geometry
          %p Discussion about para-Hermitian formalism started in
          %ul
            %li#Vaisman
              Izu Vaisman,
              %em On the geometry of double field theory
              Journal of Mathematical Physics 53, 033509 (2012) (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.0836v1"}> arXiv:1203.0836
              )
          %p Para-Hermitian formalism further developed and generalized in
          %ul
            %li#Svoboda
              David Svoboda,
              %em Algebroid structures on para-Hermitian manifolds
              Journal of Mathematical Physics 59, 122302 (2018) (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08180"}> arxiv:1802.08180
              )
          %h3#ReferencesFormalizationInHigherGeometry Formalization in higher geometry
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+field+theory"} double field theory
            using
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"} L-infinity algebra
            includes
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Deser"}> Andreas Deser
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Jim Stasheff
              = succeed "," do
                %em Even symplectic supermanifolds and double field theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1406.3601"} arXiv:1406.3601
          %p Discussion of an extended version of Riemannian geometry suitable for the description of double field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Deser"}> Andreas Deser
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Saemann"} Christian Saemann
              = succeed "," do
                %em Extended Riemannian Geometry I: Local Double Field Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.02772"} arXiv:1611.02772
          %p#ReferencesInHigherDifferentialGeometry
            Comprehensive discussion in
